Memory cap for the Apple packet tunnel: the tun path's TCP buffers.

Xray-core's gVisor stack lets every tunnelled TCP connection buffer up to
8 MiB in and 6 MiB out. A packet tunnel extension gets ~50 MiB on iOS (Apple
publishes no tvOS figure); a video app reading slowly over a few connections
fills that in seconds and the system kills the extension (docs/TVOS.md,
"Memory of the tunnel extension": 70-125 MiB measured without this patch,
22-25 MiB with it). The link between the app and the stack is local (RTT
under 1 ms), so 256 KiB per direction costs no throughput.

NOT applied by default: it changes the engine of the iPhone build too, which
is the owner's decision. For a device test:

  bash scripts/build-libxray-tvos.sh \
      --extra-patch scripts/libxray/xray-core-apple-tun-buffers.patch

LibXray.version then names it. If it ships, add it to build-libxray.sh and to
the `modifications` of Xray-core in scripts/libxray-notices.json (MPL-2.0).

diff --git a/proxy/tun/stack_gvisor.go b/proxy/tun/stack_gvisor.go
index 8584616..8055849 100644
--- a/proxy/tun/stack_gvisor.go
+++ b/proxy/tun/stack_gvisor.go
@@ -24,12 +24,12 @@ const (
 	defaultNIC tcpip.NICID = 1
 
 	tcpRXBufMinSize = tcp.MinBufferSize
-	tcpRXBufDefSize = tcp.DefaultSendBufferSize
-	tcpRXBufMaxSize = 8 << 20 // 8MiB
+	tcpRXBufDefSize = 64 << 10  // 64KiB
+	tcpRXBufMaxSize = 256 << 10 // 256KiB
 
 	tcpTXBufMinSize = tcp.MinBufferSize
-	tcpTXBufDefSize = tcp.DefaultReceiveBufferSize
-	tcpTXBufMaxSize = 6 << 20 // 6MiB
+	tcpTXBufDefSize = 64 << 10  // 64KiB
+	tcpTXBufMaxSize = 256 << 10 // 256KiB
 )
 
 // stackGVisor is ip stack implemented by gVisor package
